ncyclopedia/shashanka.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shashanka</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shashanka was a king of Gauda in Bengal in the late 6th and early 7th centuries, often described as the first ruler to unite much of Bengal.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shashe-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shashe-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shashe River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shashe is a river of southern Africa that rises in north-eastern Botswana and forms part of the border between Botswana and Zimbabwe before joining the Limpopo.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shatapatha-brahmana"><span class="enc-name">Shatapatha Brahmana</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shatapatha Brahmana ('Brahmana of a hundred paths') is a Sanskrit prose text of the White Yajurveda, composed in the first half of the 1st millennium BCE and…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shatrunjaya"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shatrunjaya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shatrunjaya</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shatrunjaya is a sacred hill above Palitana in Gujarat, India, one of the holiest pilgrimage sites of the Jains.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shatt al-Arab</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shatt al-Arab is the river formed by the joining of the Tigris and Euphrates in southern Iraq, flowing past Basra to the Persian Gulf.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shattiwaza"><span class="enc-name">Shattiwaza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shattiwaza was a king of Mitanni in the fourteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shattiwaza-treaty"><span class="enc-name">Shattiwaza treaty</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shattiwaza treaty was a treaty concluded c. 1330 BCE between the Hittite king Suppiluliuma I and Shattiwaza of Mitanni.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shattuara-i"><span class="enc-name">Shattuara I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shattuara I was a ruler of the Mitanni rump state of Hanigalbat in the thirteenth century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shattuara-ii"><span class="enc-name">Shattuara II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shattuara II was the last known king of Mitanni, or Hanigalbat, in the 13th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shatuo"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shatuo.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shatuo</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shatuo were a Turkic people of the Western Turkic tradition who settled on the northern frontier of Tang China in the 9th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaushgamuwa"><span class="enc-name">Shaushgamuwa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shaushgamuwa was a king of Amurru in the late 13th century BCE and a vassal and brother-in-law of the Hittite king Tudhaliya IV.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaushtatar"><span class="enc-name">Shaushtatar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shaushtatar was a king of Mitanni around c. 1450 BCE, under whom the kingdom reached a peak of power.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shavkat-mirziyoyev"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shavkat-mirziyoyev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shavkat Mirziyoyev</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shavkat Mirziyoyev is an Uzbek politician who became president of Uzbekistan in 2016 after the death of Islam Karimov, whose prime minister he had been since 2003.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shavsheti"><span class="enc-name">Shavsheti</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Shavsheti is a historic region of south-western Georgia, today the Şavşat district of Artvin Province in north-eastern Turkey.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shawl"><span class="enc-name">Shawl</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">A shawl is a large piece of cloth worn over the shoulders or head.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shawnee"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shawnee.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shawnee</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shawnee are an Algonquian-speaking Native American people who in the 18th century lived mainly in the Ohio country.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaybanids"><span class="enc-name">Shaybanids</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Shaybanids were an Uzbek dynasty descended from Shiban, a grandson of Genghis Khan, who ruled much of Central Asia in the sixteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-shaykh-al-mufid"><span class="enc-name">al-Shaykh al-Mufid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Shaykh al-Mufid was a Twelver Shia theologian and jurist of Baghdad and the leading Shia scholar of the Buyid period.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shaykh-haydar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shaykh Haydar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shaykh Haydar was head of the Safavid Sufi order of Ardabil from 1460 to 1488 and father of Shah Ismail I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shaykh Junayd</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shaykh Junayd was head of the Safavid Sufi order of Ardabil from 1447 to 1460.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shaykh-uways"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shaykh-uways.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shaykh Uways</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Shaykh Uways was sultan of the Jalayirid dynasty from 1356 to 1374, son of its founder Hasan Buzurg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/shays-rebellion"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/shays-rebellion.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Shays' Rebellion</span><span class="enc-type">Revolt · 1786–1787</span><span class="enc-snip">Shays' Rebellion was an armed uprising in western Massachusetts in 1786–1787 by indebted farmers, led by the war veteran Daniel Shays, against high taxes and the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/sheba-son-of-bichri"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/sheba-son-of-bichri.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Sheba son of Bichri</span><span class="e